WEBVTT

00:00:00.185 --> 00:00:03.210
A tracker never knows exactly where an
object is.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:07.535
It keeps a mean and a covariance, and the
covariance is an ellipse of doubt.

00:00:07.535 --> 00:00:12.222
Gaussians are the natural language here,
because a linear map keeps them Gaussian.

00:00:12.222 --> 00:00:15.572
The mean follows the map, and the
covariance is sandwiched.

00:00:15.572 --> 00:00:18.722
Take position and velocity, and one time
step.

00:00:18.722 --> 00:00:21.522
A round doubt shears into a tilted
ellipse:

00:00:21.522 --> 00:00:25.210
position is less certain, and it is now
tied to velocity.

00:00:25.131 --> 00:00:27.268
The second rule is conditioning.

00:00:27.268 --> 00:00:30.131
Two quantities a and b share a joint
Gaussian.

00:00:30.131 --> 00:00:32.143
Now observe b equals one.

00:00:32.143 --> 00:00:36.443
That slices the ellipse, and what's left
of a is a narrower Gaussian.

00:00:36.443 --> 00:00:40.393
The mean moves to one, and the variance
falls from four to two.

00:00:40.393 --> 00:00:44.214
The drop depends only on how strongly a
and b vary together,

00:00:44.215 --> 00:00:45.807
not on the value you saw.

00:00:45.735 --> 00:00:48.660
A Kalman filter is exactly these two
rules.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:50.010
Predict with the first:

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.422
at position one, with variance two.

00:00:52.422 --> 00:00:55.272
A measurement arrives at two, with
variance one.

00:00:55.272 --> 00:00:56.597
Condition on it.

00:00:56.597 --> 00:01:00.360
The new position is five thirds, with
variance two thirds.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.535
Now the surprise:

00:01:01.535 --> 00:01:05.597
velocity rose from one to four thirds,
though it was never measured.

00:01:05.597 --> 00:01:08.460
The tilt in the covariance linked it to
position.

00:01:08.395 --> 00:01:12.270
If the model is right, the squared
innovation over S is small.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:15.820
The usual gate for one measurement is
three point eight four.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.970
Now a measurement arrives at six.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.108
Is it accepted?

00:01:19.108 --> 00:01:20.720
Pause and work it out.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:23.017
Rejected:

00:01:23.017 --> 00:01:27.305
d squared is about eight point three, so
that detection belongs to something else.

00:01:27.305 --> 00:01:31.042
With several tracks, we must decide which
detection updates which track.

00:01:31.042 --> 00:01:32.617
Build a cost matrix.

00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:35.434
Greedy matching grabs the smallest cost,
point one,

00:01:35.435 --> 00:01:37.368
and is then forced into point nine:

00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:39.205
one point zero in total.

00:01:39.205 --> 00:01:41.830
The best pairing costs only point five.

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:44.605
The Hungarian algorithm finds it in cubic
time,

00:01:44.606 --> 00:01:49.981
by subtracting each row's minimum and each
column's minimum until independent zeros
appear.
